def lyapunov(f, x0, steps=10000, warmup=1000):
    x = x0
    s = 0.0
    for n in range(steps+warmup):
        x_next, fp = f(x)  # returns value and derivative
        if n >= warmup:
            s += math.log(abs(fp)+1e-12)
        x = x_next
    return s / steps

Chapter 1 — Cold Open (2,000 words cut to the muscle)

He should have slept hours ago. Ari Kesten, hoodie up, headphones down around his neck, watched a grid of numbers he’d never seen in the wild behave like his favorite liar.

On screen: doppler residuals from a European radar chain. They were supposed to be noisy—Gaussian, ugly, God’s honest thermal hiss. Instead, each night for a week, amid the static: a spine of ternary fractions pretending to be decimal. 0.3331… 0.6668… 0.9996… and back again. Not every minute. Not everywhere. Just enough to look like a compression artifact if compression were a sacrament.

He wrote it off the first night: bad instrument. He wrote it off the second: shared library mismatch, a rookie sysadmin somewhere. By night three he was unspooling tools he swore he’d retired when he walked out of Fort Meade, right after he refused to sign off on an encryption backdoor “for lawful access.” He’d picked principle then and lost a marriage. It still pinched.

Ari phoned Maeve.

“It’s three in the morning,” she said, but she was already typing.

“Tell me this is pareidolia.”

She didn’t. “Send me your residuals.”

He sent them. She fed his files to an operator she’d built when graduate students chewed her time like gum. On her end, a primitive transfer operator marched probability masses through a contraction map. The spectrum sharpened, narrowed, landed exactly where his fractions had lived.

She whistled. “You’re not seeing noise. You’re seeing a fixed point.”

“A fixed point of what?”

“Something that thinks ternary,” Maeve said. “And compresses as if the thing it compresses matters more than the space it saves.”

He laughed in that way that admitted fear. “So… not a codec for Netflix.”

“No.” She paused. “Ari, you ever sit with the idea the world’s digital? Cells as bits, laws as finite automata?”

“And spend ten years proving to myself it couldn’t hold unless the bit depth was—”

“Analog,” Maeve finished. “Right. What if someone built a compressor for analog truth?”

He wanted to make a joke. He didn’t. He saw his coffee mug tremble, remembered he hadn’t touched the desk.

“Okay,” he said. “So where do you point your probe if you want the compressor to blink first?”

“Second order,” she said. “Not the gates themselves—their neighborhoods.”

By dawn a liaison named Rao was on his porch with a folder and a polite threat. He had a lab. He had funding. He had legal coverage that smelled like gasoline and matches. Ari signed anyway.

That evening he took Tamar, the cat, off the keyboard for the fifth time and started building the world’s pettiest heresy: two analog computers in software, both fed the same field, both composed to second order, identical in every parameter but one. One built from NAND, the other from NOR.

If the universe truly averaged causation over neighborhoods, then near the threshold 
𝜏
τ the two would disagree—not everywhere, not always, but right where the compressor was cheapest and sloppiest. In the ambiguity band. Where a nudge could become a note someone would hear.

He kept the first nudge small: a crosswalk signal downtown that tended to stick on DON’T WALK during lunch. He didn’t force it to flip. He merely made the NOR neighborhood slightly heavier than the NAND one, a bias of a few percent, applied at 12:13 local, for one cycle.

At 12:13:07 the signal blinked WALK, early, and a woman carrying a cello case crossed smiling, as if fate had finally learned manners.

Ari grinned like a thief. Then the line at the cafe around the corner erupted in curses as the payment terminals all hiccupped at once, resetting and voiding orders. Ten minutes of outage across six blocks. No pattern. Crudely nonlocal.

Maeve marked it and underlined it twice. “Checksum,” she said. “You made it easier over here. It got harder over there.”

Rao, watching, did not grin. “If we can route compensation into places that don’t matter…”

“Places always matter to someone,” Maeve said, flat as a freeway.

No one slept that night.

