τ hung between them like a dare.

He checked the window. Brussels summer. It had been warm and indecisive all week. He cracked the sash; the night kissed his knuckles with ordinary air. He closed it again, felt stupid, reopened it, told himself it was for the GPU.

He coded his Lyapunov probe. He tuned the dials from 0.0 to 1.0 in tenths. He set up the overlay—cyan for NAND, yellow for NOR—and aligned their traces on a virtual scope.

“Ten bucks says you get bored,” he told Tamar. She stared like a librarian.

The first hour the plots lay on each other so perfectly he wanted to use them as a pillow. The second hour they began to separate, just a hair, then loop back together, then separate again with a sleepy patience that said the kernel was breathing. The fans ramped, a soft wave. A dead water bottle on the floor gave a quiet pop.

He ran the probe again. The Lyapunov exponent nudged above zero and went sulky. Phase drift, not chaos. He drank something blue and chemical and regretted it instantly.

At 03:17, the city outside changed its mind. Sirens that had been background salt rose to a wail that lived everywhere at once. He tried to count distinct sources and failed. Tamar hissed at nothing in particular and loped out of the room, tail like a question mark.

The cyan and yellow traces did something he had never seen. They stopped pretending to be friends and began to cross—first in a neat plus, then with a bulge, then with the fat halo of an icon he did not want to name.

He didn’t breathe. The cross resolved to a letter. Then another. Then a script that his grandmother had made him learn on Sunday afternoons when the world was small: ו ת ר ס. Vav. Tav. Resh. Samekh. His mouth said the shapes before his mind admitted them.

He shook himself like a dog. “I’m not doing mysticism,” he told no one. Somewhere below the sirens a sub-bass swelled as if the city were a concert hall warming up in E.

He leaned into the stupidest detail: his knobs. He’d been twiddling them in decimals out of habit. The residuals had been ternary. Maeve’s contraction map had been ternary. He set the dial snapping function to thirds and two-thirds. He went parameter by parameter, snapping 0.7 to 2/3, 0.3 to 1/3, cursing his own laziness under his breath.

The virtual scope got bored of being pious. The crucifix melted to an “M,” then to something like a comb, then to a steady row of narrow teeth that did not look at all like salvation.

The fans screamed now. The bottle on the floor inverted its dents. He pulled a little acrylic gadget from a drawer—his nixie clock on a USB lead—and stuffed it into a port. HELLO, it said in orange tubes, then TIME, then TEMP: 14.3 C. July had not signed up for that.

14.2. 14.1. 14.0.

He made his voice bland. “We’re in the band,” he told Maeve on an open line.

“Then get out of it,” she said, too fast. “If you pushed it into bifurcation—”

“It’s not bifurcating,” he said. “It’s—compensating.”

“For what?”

“For me being clever.”

The sirens were either everywhere or nowhere. He couldn’t tell which. The GPU was a hurricane. He put a finger over the last dial, the one that would snap from 0.66 to 2/3 and make the overlay perfect in every way that mattered to a god or a custodian.

He locked the knob.

The house shifted. Not a jolt—a tilt, like a giant exhaled under the foundation. He ran for the stairs, caught Tamar underfoot, apologized to gravity as he pancaked on the landing. He flung the front door open and saw—

Language took a number and waited.

Later, he would say he remembered only two things: the sound a cat makes when it despises the physics currently on offer, and the rotor-beat of a Chinook turning the night air into a set of metronomes. The helicopter settled on his street like an argument punctuated with a full stop.

The sirens stopped. The nixie said 1.2 C and falling. He watched the temperature tick down as men in cold-weather uniforms that did not have agency names escorted him, gently, like he was made of eggs.

Inside the helicopter, Maeve was already buckled in, eyes bright, lips bloodless. Rao didn’t smile, didn’t scowl. He held Ari’s gaze a second too long and then looked past him at nothing.

“Can we agree,” Rao said into his headset mic as the ground slid away, “that there are operational security concerns?”

Ari stared down at his neighborhood, which looked perfectly ordinary again except that it wore the memory of a crucifix.

“Sure,” he said. “Let’s start with the part where I almost stapled the sim to a desk.”

Tamar, in his hoodie now like a stolen loaf of bread, purred at a frequency that made everything feel three degrees warmer.

