They staged a failure. The lab’s power supply blew—loudly, convincingly. They published a paper on ternary compression with all the teeth filed down and then pretended to hate each other in public. Rao redeployed. The world kept its noise and its crosswalks.

At night, Ari tuned his knobs to thirds. He never pushed a dial to the lock again. Tamar slept on the equipment rack like she’d always intended to.

Sometimes, when the weather did something that made the news anchors wear their concerned faces, Ari would stand by the window and think about checksum. He’d rehearse the Psalm he didn’t believe in. Though he falls, he will not be cast down. Then he would add, quietly, like a patch note to the universe: If and only if the kernel closes.

He closed it.

The compressor hummed. Reality, mercifully, stayed lossy.

